GRC Engineering Development Rules

General Approach

Follow the instructions in the root AGENTS.md and the implementation plan in docs/BUILD_PLAN.md.

Treat this as a GRC Engineering portfolio project that must demonstrate working logic, traceability, repeatable testing, and documented limitations.

Keep changes small, focused, and reviewable.

Before making substantial changes, inspect the relevant files and explain the proposed implementation.

Do not implement future phases prematurely.

Never claim a feature, test, validation, or deployment succeeded unless it actually did.

Architecture

Keep risk evaluation, evidence validation, control mapping, workflow metrics, and cost calculations separate from Flask route handlers.

Keep database access separate from business logic wherever practical.

Use Flask routes to validate requests, call application services, and return results.

The dashboard must display results produced by the application services.

Do not calculate or invent risk decisions independently in the frontend.

Prefer readable, focused Python functions with clear inputs and outputs.

Avoid introducing unnecessary frameworks, dependencies, services, or abstractions.

Risk Evaluation

Use deterministic, explicit rules for risk evaluation.

Identical assessment inputs evaluated with the same rule-set version must produce identical outputs.

Keep inherent risk, evidence confidence, and residual risk distinct.

Explain every material risk factor and escalation.

Keep weights, thresholds, and mandatory review conditions in version-controlled configuration.

Record the rule-set version used for each assessment.

Missing information must not silently count as evidence of a safeguard.

Missing evidence alone does not prove that a control has failed.

Distinguish unknown, self-attested, verified, conflicting, expired, and confirmed-failure conditions.

A low aggregate score must never bypass a configured mandatory escalation.

Do not automatically approve a vendor solely because its score is low.

Do not use an external LLM to make unreviewed risk-scoring or approval decisions.

Label the initial model as illustrative until validated against an approved organizational methodology.

Evidence Validation

Model a vendor claim separately from its evidence artifacts and validation result.

A vendor assertion is not equivalent to independently verified evidence.

Keep evidence status and assessment conclusions separate.

Support verified, self-attested, missing, conflicting, expired, and not-yet-reviewed states where applicable.

Apply evidence freshness rules by evidence type and documented policy.

Do not assume that every evidence artifact expires after the same interval.

A file hash may help identify changes but does not prove authenticity, accuracy, completeness, or sufficiency.

Preserve the relationship among assessments, claims, evidence, findings, and control mappings.

Control Mapping

Do not invent framework identifiers, requirement wording, citations, or source references.

Verify mappings against appropriate authoritative sources before marking them reviewed.

Include framework name, version, requirement identifier, applicability, rationale, source reference, and review status.

Label unverified mappings as draft or unverified.

Distinguish direct requirements from interpretive cross-framework mappings.

Do not imply that mapping a requirement proves equivalence, complete conformity, or certification.

Do not imply the application certifies an organization or vendor.

Keep mappings small and defensible rather than maximizing the number of controls listed.

Risk Register and Remediation

Make risks traceable to relevant assessments, claims, evidence gaps, and findings.

Keep inherent risk and residual risk separate.

Record meaningful explanations for risk dispositions and exceptions.

Link remediation actions to the risks or findings they address.

Track owners, due dates, status, and completion information where applicable.

Record meaningful workflow events.

Do not describe a local database event log as immutable or tamper-proof.

Treat ServiceNow integration as simulated until a real integration is explicitly approved and implemented.

Cost and Workflow Metrics

Distinguish measured data from illustrative assumptions.

Distinguish human handling time from elapsed cycle time.

Distinguish labor capacity value from actual cash savings.

Distinguish baseline effort from assisted-workflow effort.

Do not claim cost savings unless supported by comparable measurements or explicitly labeled assumptions.

Include rework and ongoing maintenance effort when supported by measurements or clearly disclosed model inputs.

Do not monetize hypothetical avoided security incidents in the initial MVP.

Make every financial figure traceable to a formula and its inputs.

Do not hardcode convincing-looking dashboard metrics that are not derived from the application data or explicitly labeled demo data.

Testing

Write pytest tests for business logic independently of Flask routes and frontend behavior.

Test normal, negative, boundary, missing-data, conflicting-data, and escalation cases as appropriate.

Test the three defined demo scenarios against expected outcomes.

Test cost arithmetic and relevant edge cases.

Add or update tests whenever business logic changes.

Never remove or weaken a test solely to make the suite pass.

Run the relevant tests after implementation and the full suite before declaring a phase complete.

Report actual test commands and outcomes.

If a test fails, explain the failure and investigate it rather than concealing it.

Security and Privacy

Use synthetic demo data.

Never add credentials, API keys, access tokens, passwords, production records, or private evidence to source control.

Keep local databases, .env files, virtual environments, and generated runtime artifacts out of Git.

Validate untrusted input at application boundaries.

Use parameterized SQL queries or equivalent safe database APIs.

Do not expose debug mode or development secrets in public deployment.

Do not upload private evidence to an external AI service.

Do not deploy, change DNS, create cloud resources, delete data, or make consequential external changes without approval.

Do not equate passing basic application tests with production security.

Documentation and Change Review

Keep the README and relevant documents synchronized with implemented behavior.

Document risk-model assumptions, control mapping methodology, cost formulas, data handling, and limitations.

After each phase, report the files changed, tests executed and their actual results, known limitations, and the recommended next step.

Review the Git diff before committing changes.

Avoid unrelated rewrites and preserve working functionality.

Do not claim that unimplemented future features already exist.

Portfolio Quality

A successful feature must be explainable to a technical reviewer.

Be prepared to explain:

What problem the feature addresses

How the logic works

Why the decision rules exist

What evidence supports the output

How the feature was tested

What its limitations are

What would need to change before production use

The objective is trustworthy, repeatable GRC automation—not merely a polished dashboard.